kirbwarrior said:
I'm still waiting to hear them (but not saying that has to happen in this thread or site). From what I can tell, anyone who is very pro-AI comes dangerously close to breaking rule 1.2.
Click to expand...
Well... For starters, AI has the potential to have much larger base of knowledge than humans ever could. This has applications not only in programming and art, but also in other spheres of knowledge. It could help us understand DNA or peptides and peptide structures.
If we however limit ourselves only to programming and art, since that's what seems to bother everybody:
Currently, moral/ethical issues have varying relevance based on your moral compass, because we've yet to determine whether training AI is IP theft, or fair use. Both sides have valid points. If I'm to summarize them both:
Anti-AI:
- AI companies took large amounts of copyrighted data without permission or reimbursement)
- GANs can reproduce the original data pretty closely or, in certain cases, identically
- AI can, through the industrial power of its production, distill the value of production. Art could suddenly become very cheap.
Pro-AI:
- AI companies took large amounts of data. So much that it has been transformed into a generic concept and if you were to examine the data, you wouldn't find a trace of original data anymore.
- The use of the training data is transformative, since the companies themselves used the data to create a GAN model, not create images. And any subsequent product comes as a result of such data.
- The data it scraped was publicly accessible (well, with the exception of Github, which has used code from private repositories to train its copilot)
- Humans learn the same way. We analyze and reproduce copyrighted examples.
Arguments aren't however just limited to the scope of copyright. To name a few:
Anti-AI:
- AI can (and has been) abused for naughty stuff. One very nasty example I could speak about is deepfake.
- You cannot copyright AI generated work
Pro-AI:
- GANs make art, programming and other things more accessible, because if you can master prompt engineering, then sky is the limit.
- If ultimately legality becomes the only real problem preventing AI from existing, it's an easy thing to remedy. There are already AI models trained on public domain, CC-0 and opt-in obtained data.
Note: I have summarized them, but I will not discuss them. At least for now. I don't want this to get overly emotional.
TRIDIUM said:
Genuine question, how would you say it consistently assists you? In a way that you couldn't achieve by referring to manuals, tutorials, googling etc.
Click to expand...
I can 100% answer that. Lemme pull up my code.
Just to keep it short, here
Spoiler
Code:
//Resolutions:
//1024x768
//1152x720
//1280x720
//1280x800 //steam deck, not sure if I should even support it
//1366x768
//1440x900
//1600x900
//1920x1080

switch (file[0]) {
            0 => {
                this._res_width = 1024;
                this._res_height = 768;
            },
            1 => {
                this._res_width = 1152;
                this._res_height = 720;
            },
            2 => {
                this._res_width = 1280;
                this._res_height = 720;
            },
            3 => {
                this._res_width = 1280;
                this._res_height = 800;
            },
            4 => {
                this._res_width = 1366;
                this._res_height = 768;
            },
            5 => {
                this._res_width = 1440;
                this._res_height = 900;
            },
            6 => {
                this._res_width = 1600;
                this._res_height = 900;
            },
            7 => {
                this._res_width = 1920;
                this._res_height = 1080;
            },
            else => {},
        }
is some code I used to test how well the AI infers stuff from comments.
I simply wrote a couple of resolutions as a comment and then proceeded to type this switch statement.
How much did I type?
Only the "0" segment.
It finished segments 1-7 completely on its own. All I did was press Tab twice.
In this particular case, it isn't relevant - it could have been done in many ways which wouldn't require much typing. But I've had cases where there was no other way. For instance, making a char interpreter.
In general, it saves me a lot of time when I have with repetitive tasks: switches, if cases, similar functions, debug logs and stuff like that. It also saves me a lot of time when I'm refactoring, because it makes far more complex suggestions than search & replace ever could. I can delete entire function segments with ease. If I'm making a function and I define something in another file (or even redefine it), it instantly knows that it should suggest that. All I do is hit Tab.
It may not look like much, but the amount of typing it saves me, I can't appreciate enough. I hate typing as much as I normally would, because it's annoying when I have to type repetitive things and my typing is not foolproof. I'm entirely capable of producing subtle bugs that I have problems finding. Refactoring was a chore, now it isn't so much anymore. If I could pay for such a tool prior to having AI, I would. Because nowadays, I actually have fun while I'm programming. Which is a welcome change.

